WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.862
In this video you'll learn what the Cyborgism post

2
00:00:04.862 --> 00:00:08.578
means by working inside a loop with a base model,

3
00:00:08.578 --> 00:00:12.200
and how to try a small Loom on this page.

4
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.081
In the cyborg way of working,

5
00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:17.076
the human keeps the goal and the judgment

6
00:00:17.076 --> 00:00:20.106
while the model supplies the breadth;

7
00:00:20.106 --> 00:00:23.067
Mini Loom lets you feel that loop

8
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:26.376
with real trees grown in advance.

9
00:00:26.376 --> 00:00:29.011
The ideas come from janus,

10
00:00:29.011 --> 00:00:33.980
who posts as @repligate, and from Cyborgism,

11
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.220
by Nicholas Kees Dupuis and janus.

12
00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.500
Cyborgism, from February 2023, responds to plans

13
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.180
to hand research to automated AI assistants:

14
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.338
agents that take a goal and work on their own.

15
00:00:50.338 --> 00:00:52.671
The authors list what base models

16
00:00:52.671 --> 00:00:54.239
lack for that job:

17
00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:58.337
a steady goal, coherence over long stretches,

18
00:00:58.337 --> 00:01:00.752
staying grounded in reality.

19
00:01:00.752 --> 00:01:02.865
Filling those gaps, they argue,

20
00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:06.151
is a step toward the dangerous kind of system.

21
00:01:06.151 --> 00:01:08.623
Their alternative is the cyborg:

22
00:01:08.623 --> 00:01:11.956
a person in a fast loop with a base model,

23
00:01:11.956 --> 00:01:14.429
using tools built for it.

24
00:01:14.429 --> 00:01:17.029
The human supplies the missing pieces,

25
00:01:17.029 --> 00:01:18.980
direction and judgment;

26
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.430
the model supplies variation,

27
00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.937
many continuations at once.

28
00:01:23.937 --> 00:01:27.153
In the post's words, the tools should "extend

29
00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:30.810
human agency rather than replace it."

30
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.910
The aim was to speed up alignment research,

31
00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.952
the work of making AI systems safe.

32
00:01:38.402 --> 00:01:41.119
The picture underneath comes from janus's

33
00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:46.053
earlier post Simulators, from September 2022.

34
00:01:46.053 --> 00:01:47.841
A base model, she argues,

35
00:01:47.841 --> 00:01:51.022
is not one character with goals of its own.

36
00:01:51.022 --> 00:01:53.124
It simulates whatever

37
00:01:53.124 --> 00:01:55.527
could have written the text so far:

38
00:01:55.527 --> 00:02:00.554
a novelist, a forum thread, a manual, a lecture.

39
00:02:00.554 --> 00:02:02.621
She calls the model the simulator,

40
00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:05.639
and the characters it produces simulacra.

41
00:02:05.639 --> 00:02:08.252
The prompt sets up a world:

42
00:02:08.252 --> 00:02:09.703
what kind of document,

43
00:02:09.703 --> 00:02:12.304
who is speaking, what has happened.

44
00:02:12.304 --> 00:02:14.811
Everything after it tends to fit,

45
00:02:14.811 --> 00:02:18.447
but a prompt rarely pins down one world.

46
00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:20.825
In Mini Loom's lighthouse story,

47
00:02:20.825 --> 00:02:24.308
an isolated keeper's radio crackles to life.

48
00:02:24.308 --> 00:02:27.315
In one branch the voice might ask for help;

49
00:02:27.315 --> 00:02:30.311
in another, it might know the keeper's name.

50
00:02:30.311 --> 00:02:34.073
Each branch is a world the same prompt allows.

51
00:02:34.073 --> 00:02:36.778
Weaving is choosing among them.

52
00:02:36.778 --> 00:02:39.924
In her testimony, curation controls "which

53
00:02:39.924 --> 00:02:44.558
hallucinated situation gets lazily rendered."

54
00:02:45.007 --> 00:02:47.991
Mini Loom runs in your browser, on this page.

55
00:02:47.991 --> 00:02:51.009
Its trees were grown in advance by a real

56
00:02:51.009 --> 00:02:56.675
base model, Qwen3, with 1.7 billion parameters.

57
00:02:56.675 --> 00:02:58.091
The reading pane shows

58
00:02:58.091 --> 00:03:00.715
your current path as one document,

59
00:03:00.715 --> 00:03:03.966
each chosen piece faintly marked.

60
00:03:03.966 --> 00:03:06.218
Under it are four futures.

61
00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:08.157
Each card shows its text,

62
00:03:08.157 --> 00:03:11.582
a bar for how expected its words were, on average,

63
00:03:11.582 --> 00:03:15.796
and the odds of the whole passage, usually tiny.

64
00:03:15.796 --> 00:03:17.526
Click one, or press its number,

65
00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:19.291
and it joins the page.

66
00:03:19.291 --> 00:03:23.796
Token view tints every token by its probability;

67
00:03:23.796 --> 00:03:27.789
tap one to see what else the model considered.

68
00:03:27.789 --> 00:03:29.961
The map draws the whole tree,

69
00:03:29.961 --> 00:03:31.923
with your path in gold.

70
00:03:31.923 --> 00:03:34.128
Click any node to jump there.

71
00:03:34.128 --> 00:03:36.532
The meter counts your bits of selection

72
00:03:36.532 --> 00:03:38.448
against the model's words.

73
00:03:38.448 --> 00:03:42.256
If you want, a much smaller live model downloads

74
00:03:42.256 --> 00:03:45.982
on request and grows branches from any node,

75
00:03:45.982 --> 00:03:48.933
or from your own prompt.

76
00:03:49.383 --> 00:03:51.032
Four things to try.

77
00:03:51.032 --> 00:03:54.190
One: pick the most surprising branch,

78
00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.185
the one the model expected least, every time,

79
00:03:57.185 --> 00:03:59.902
and watch how far the text wanders.

80
00:03:59.902 --> 00:04:01.991
Two: go back to the root

81
00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:04.326
and take a different first branch.

82
00:04:04.326 --> 00:04:06.868
Same prompt, a different world.

83
00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:09.678
Three: find a narrow place in the tree,

84
00:04:09.678 --> 00:04:11.535
where the four futures say nearly

85
00:04:11.535 --> 00:04:14.031
the same thing, and a wide one.

86
00:04:14.031 --> 00:04:18.211
Four: compare your bits with the model's words.

87
00:04:18.211 --> 00:04:21.775
Each choice among four adds two bits;

88
00:04:21.775 --> 00:04:25.049
the model writes every word in between.

89
00:04:25.049 --> 00:04:28.973
Every branch point you pass is one sampled token.

90
00:04:28.973 --> 00:04:31.318
Part two goes inside the model,

91
00:04:31.318 --> 00:04:34.140
to what happens between one token and the next,

92
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.274
and why that sampled token is the only thing

93
00:04:37.274 --> 00:04:39.981
that travels back down to the bottom.

94
00:04:40.431 --> 00:04:43.217
Keep this. One.

95
00:04:43.217 --> 00:04:45.005
The human steers and the model

96
00:04:45.005 --> 00:04:46.584
supplies the breadth:

97
00:04:46.584 --> 00:04:48.755
you keep the goal and the judgment,

98
00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:52.436
a base model offers the possibilities.

99
00:04:52.436 --> 00:04:55.988
Two. A prompt allows many worlds:

100
00:04:55.988 --> 00:04:58.833
janus describes a base model as a simulator,

101
00:04:58.833 --> 00:05:01.366
and each branch is one of those worlds.

102
00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:04.023
Three. Weaving is reading,

103
00:05:04.023 --> 00:05:05.729
choosing and going back;

104
00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:08.691
Mini Loom lets you try it and count your bits.
